[{:db/id 17592186147425 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3582cc5d-9e46-5e0a-8d0c-b0e74f8f5924" "question-caefbcdd-6450-5b4b-b965-772f5b87b02a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3582cc5d-9e46-5e0a-8d0c-b0e74f8f5924" :question/id #uuid "3582cc5d-9e46-5e0a-8d0c-b0e74f8f5924" :question/math-academy-id "q-71349" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-1=0$ and $x+z+1=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+y-1=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+1=0\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩+t⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+(-1)=0,$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee9b9e23-e285-51ba-8f2e-0f93320571fc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee9b9e23-e285-51ba-8f2e-0f93320571fc" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9b9e23-e285-51ba-8f2e-0f93320571fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77d58298-86f5-5da7-a6f5-5aadeb1af6ca" "answer-d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" "answer-782abb1a-40ca-50f3-9eb2-460b14bea776" "answer-8b4a733d-fbda-5f24-ba39-11bd9f15b6aa" "answer-5a5b51a0-3005-5ddd-b5ab-b8d3b530fe56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77d58298-86f5-5da7-a6f5-5aadeb1af6ca" :answer/id #uuid "77d58298-86f5-5da7-a6f5-5aadeb1af6ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-1,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" :answer/id #uuid "d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-782abb1a-40ca-50f3-9eb2-460b14bea776" :answer/id #uuid "782abb1a-40ca-50f3-9eb2-460b14bea776" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b4a733d-fbda-5f24-ba39-11bd9f15b6aa" :answer/id #uuid "8b4a733d-fbda-5f24-ba39-11bd9f15b6aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a5b51a0-3005-5ddd-b5ab-b8d3b530fe56" :answer/id #uuid "5a5b51a0-3005-5ddd-b5ab-b8d3b530fe56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338949 :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n\nII. The planes are parallel.\n\nIII. The planes intersect on a line." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413744 :question/problem "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nII. The planes intersect on a line.\n\nIII. The planes are coincident." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-caefbcdd-6450-5b4b-b965-772f5b87b02a" :question/id #uuid "caefbcdd-6450-5b4b-b965-772f5b87b02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129208" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x+3y-3=0$ and $3x+z+19=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+3y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+19=0\\,⟹\\,z=-19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-19⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3y-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+z+19=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-2j-9k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-19⟩+t⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=1-(-19)=20,$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1ea7962c-6925-55c2-86b0-52523c308e6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1ea7962c-6925-55c2-86b0-52523c308e6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea7962c-6925-55c2-86b0-52523c308e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e23fcb96-7207-5675-97d0-1ce03137e6ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0bc5a66d-b1b1-585a-b0bc-21d19ca71687" "answer-9c3c13